<html>
<title>Debugger</title>
<body>

<h1>Debugger</h1>

<p>
The Debugger tab provides debugging functionality for both RCA CDP1802 machine code and pseudo-code interpreters.
</p>

<p>
The main elements of the Debugger tab are shown in the screenshot below.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/debugger.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
To open this tab, select the <strong>Debugger</strong> tab (1).
</p>

<p>
The Debugger tab contains two sub-tabs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(2) <strong>1802</strong> sub-tab - debugging of RCA CDP1802 machine code. See the <a href="DebuggerTrace.htm">CDP1802 Debugger</a> section.</li>
<li>(3) <strong>Pseudo</strong> sub-tab - debugging of pseudo-code interpreters such as Chip-8, ST2, ST4, FEL-1, CARDTRAN, etc. See the <a href="DebuggerChip8.htm">Pseudo Debugger</a> section.</li>
</ul>


<h2>Important Notes</h2>

<ul>
<li>Settings in the <strong>1802</strong> and <strong>Pseudo</strong> tabs are <strong>not</strong> saved in the Emma 02 configuration file. Each time Emma 02 starts, debugger settings return to their default values.</li>

<li>SCRT definitions and other options can be configured in the emulator XML configuration files.</li>

</ul>


<h2>When to Use the Debugger</h2>

<p>
Use the debugger when:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Your program crashes or behaves unexpectedly.</li>
<li>You want to understand how a program works.</li>
<li>You need to check register or memory values.</li>
<li>You are learning CDP1802 assembly or pseudo-code systems.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The debugger is one of the most powerful tools in Emma 02.
Start with simple programs, step through instructions slowly, and watch how the machine state changes.
</p>

</body>
</html>


